Computer Games at Work

One-quarter of the people who play computer games 
played their last PC-based game at work.

A new study by Coleman & Associates, Inc., a Teaneck, 
New Jersey market research firm specializing in high-
tech research, has found that 44% of the United States 
population play games on their Pcs.  Half the men in 
the country play (51%), and nearly two-fifths of the 
women (38%).

These adult games players, particularly the women, are 
disporportionately heads of households, who work, 
either full- or part-time.  Thus, it is perhaps not 
surprising that when asked their �most recent 
experience with playing a computer game,� 23% reported 
they played their last game at the office.

Women, however, play computer games at the office in 
significantly greater proportions than men do:  27% of 
the women played their last game at work, while only 
19% of the men did.

But the gender differences are not necessarily a 
reflection of the stereotyped secretarial pool versus 
professional management personnel.  In fact, middle 
management, people making between $40,000 and $50,000 a 
year, played notably more games at work than any other 
income category:  38% as compared to 25% of the $25-
40,000 group, 19% of the less than $25,000 group, or 
17% of the $50,000+ group.

And it is not only middle management, bu the middle-
aged - people between 45 and 54 - who most often played 
their last game at the office:  29%.  The oldest 
workers are the most diligent; onlyl 12% of the 55-64 
year old computer game players played their last game 
at fwork.  Twenty-three percent of those 18-34 and 23% 
of those 35-44 used company time for computer games.

Finally, work time computer game playing is most 
popular in the South, with 28% playing their last game 
at the office.  Only 17% in the North East, 18% in the 
West, and 23% in the North Central region played at 
work.

This nationwide study was conducted in January 1995 
among 1003 adults, using a random digit dialing, last 
birthday telephone interview methodology.  The results 
are statistically significant at the 95% confidence 
level (+/- 3.5%).

